10208.sol:
Equation of a tangent to a parabola $y^2=4 x$ is

y=m x+$\frac{1}{m}$ Since, it passes through point $(1,4)$. So,
or

4=m+$\frac{1}{m}

\begin{array}{ll}
\Rightarrow& m²-4 m+1 \\
\therefore & m=\frac{4 \pm \sqrt{16-4}}{2}=\frac{4 \pm \sqrt{12}}{2}\\
& m=2 \pm \sqrt{3}\\
\therefore & m_1=2+\sqrt{3} \text{ and }m_2=2-\sqrt{3}
\end{array}$ Hence, angle between two slopes is

$\begin{aligned}\tan\theta& =\left|\frac{m_1-m_2}{1+m_1 m_2}\right|=\left|\frac{2 \sqrt{3}}{1+1}\right|\\
\Rightarrow \quad \tan \theta& =\sqrt{3}
\end{aligned}\begin{aligned} \Rightarrow & \tan \theta & =\tan \frac{\pi}{3} \\ \Rightarrow & \theta & =\frac{\pi}{3}\end{aligned}$

